stdcallpop_pe_i386.exe -- the x86 Windows witness for `calleepop`
(`kuna_decomp::p6_variables::kuna_calleepop`).

What it is
  A 40,960-byte 32-bit MSVC Windows GUI crackme. It matters because it is an
  ordinary `x86:LE:32:default:windows` image whose logic calls Win32 APIs
  through the Import Address Table in the usual `__stdcall` way, with one
  `__cdecl` varargs import (`wsprintfA`) mixed in and internal `__cdecl` helpers
  around it -- the three conventions kuna has to tell apart with no type
  information at all.

  The function at 0x401290 is the one the regression probe reads. It calls
  `ReadFile` with a pointer to a four-byte stack slot, then checksums the same
  slot afterwards. With the callee-pop guess wrong that one slot is modelled at
  two different offsets, so the checksum reads a variable nothing writes and the
  buffer prints as a raw `&Stackffffffe0`.

Why it is vendored rather than built
  No MSVC targets the Linux CI image, and neither parity corpus can carry the
  defect: both are symbol-less byte chunks, and the mechanism is gated on
  `Varnode::externref` -- a fact that only exists once a real PE import
  directory has been parsed.

Where it came from
  The RE-friction loop's round-6 witness: challenge 5ab77f5d33c5d40ad448c6f6 in
  the kuna-re-dataset crackme corpus ("imagination by kratorius",
  https://crackmes.one/crackme/5ab77f5d33c5d40ad448c6f6),
  `bin/Imagination.zip.__x/Imagination.exe`, byte-identical. That is the binary
  on which a tester found the pixel reader using undeclared stack objects and
  filed docs/re-needs/pixel-reader-uses-undefined.md.

  sha256 78d28ec6d13b9c1634101e86b2042ed61560db31ba63705adfb2b75f5757b7b4
  It is a third-party crackme, included unmodified as test data only.
